1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,394
Welcome to lesson twenty-four, the final lesson.

2
00:00:03,494 --> 00:00:18,000
You'll step back from single clusters: sharing clusters safely between teams, running fleets across regions, planning disaster recovery, controlling cost, and building an internal platform that makes Kubernetes easy and safe for developers.

3
00:00:19,350 --> 00:00:31,232
Why run several clusters? To limit the blast radius of a bad change, to separate environments, to serve users in several regions, to meet data residency rules, and to isolate untrusted tenants.

4
00:00:31,332 --> 00:00:37,326
But every cluster needs its own upgrades, add-ons and attention, and small clusters waste capacity.

5
00:00:37,426 --> 00:00:43,996
Most organizations mix a few large, well-governed shared clusters with dedicated ones where isolation demands it.

6
00:00:45,346 --> 00:00:47,964
Sharing a cluster is called multi-tenancy.

7
00:00:48,064 --> 00:00:55,978
Soft multi-tenancy is for teams in the same company. The goal is to prevent accidents and noisy neighbors, using namespaces and guardrails.

8
00:00:56,078 --> 00:01:06,360
Hard multi-tenancy is for customers or untrusted code, where tenants might be malicious. That needs separate clusters, virtual clusters, or sandboxed runtimes.

9
00:01:07,710 --> 00:01:14,216
For soft multi-tenancy, apply a tenant template to every team namespace. It combines everything you've learned.

10
00:01:14,316 --> 00:01:29,350
Access through a role binding to the team's group. A resource quota for a fair share, and a limit range for defaults. Default-deny network policies. The restricted Pod security standard. Admission policies, an Argo CD project, and cost labels.

11
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:33,206
Namespaces don't isolate everything.

12
00:01:33,306 --> 00:01:44,260
Cluster-scoped resources like CRDs are shared, so two teams needing different versions conflict. All Pods on a node share the kernel. And one tenant can overload the API server.

13
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:56,194
Virtual clusters help: each tenant gets its own API server and can even be cluster admin, while their Pods actually run in one namespace of the host. But a virtual cluster doesn't isolate the kernel.

14
00:01:57,544 --> 00:02:03,458
To manage a fleet, create clusters as code, with Terraform, Cluster API or Crossplane.

15
00:02:03,558 --> 00:02:12,048
Give every cluster the same platform baseline: networking, certificates, ingress, secrets, monitoring, policies and backups.

16
00:02:12,148 --> 00:02:20,094
And deliver it with GitOps. An ApplicationSet with a cluster generator can install the baseline on every production cluster automatically.

17
00:02:21,444 --> 00:02:29,518
Global load balancing sends users to the right cluster, and tools like the Multi-Cluster Services API connect services across clusters.

18
00:02:29,618 --> 00:02:35,068
The hard part is data. Stateless services are easy to run in several regions. Data isn't.

19
00:02:35,168 --> 00:02:42,474
Active-passive is simpler but loses some time and possibly some data on failover. Active-active needs databases designed for it.

20
00:02:42,574 --> 00:02:47,512
Agree with the business on the recovery time objective and recovery point objective.

21
00:02:48,862 --> 00:02:52,840
Disaster recovery strategies trade cost against recovery time.

22
00:02:52,940 --> 00:03:06,038
Backup and restore is cheapest but takes hours. A pilot light keeps data replicated with a minimal standby. A warm standby runs a scaled-down copy. Active-active recovers almost instantly, at the highest cost.

23
00:03:06,138 --> 00:03:11,140
Whatever you choose, test it. Run regular game days where you actually fail over.

24
00:03:12,490 --> 00:03:23,364
Kubernetes is powerful but complex. Platform engineering gives developers an internal platform with golden paths: the easy, supported, secure-by-default way to build and run software.

25
00:03:23,464 --> 00:03:34,578
A developer picks a template in a portal like Backstage, and gets a repository with CI, a guarded namespace, a database through a Crossplane claim, dashboards and alerts, all through GitOps.

26
00:03:34,678 --> 00:03:39,472
The first deploy happens in minutes, with no tickets, and every guardrail applied.

27
00:03:40,822 --> 00:03:45,232
A good platform is treated as a product, with developers as its customers.

28
00:03:45,332 --> 00:03:53,374
Make the right way the easiest way, but allow documented exceptions. Offer self-service instead of tickets, with security built in.

29
00:03:53,474 --> 00:04:03,036
Hide what developers don't need, but never hide what they need to debug. Start small, and measure outcomes, like the DORA metrics and developer satisfaction.

30
00:04:04,386 --> 00:04:07,276
Kubernetes makes it easy to waste money invisibly.

31
00:04:07,376 --> 00:04:14,810
Allocate cost to teams by resource requests, because requests reserve capacity. Right-size them from real usage.

32
00:04:14,910 --> 00:04:26,296
Autoscale everything that can be, use spot instances for work that tolerates interruption, scale down non-production at night, and watch hidden costs like idle load balancers and log volume.

33
00:04:27,646 --> 00:04:35,960
In the lab, you'll build a tenant template and prove the isolation between two teams: access, Pod security, quota and network.

34
00:04:36,060 --> 00:04:44,534
You'll give a third team a virtual cluster where they're cluster admin, apply a baseline to a small fleet, and produce a cost report by namespace.

35
00:04:45,884 --> 00:04:47,942
Let's recap the final lesson.

36
00:04:48,042 --> 00:04:55,588
Mix shared, governed clusters with dedicated ones. Give every team a tenant template, and remember what namespaces can't isolate.

37
00:04:55,688 --> 00:05:02,594
Manage fleets as code with a GitOps baseline, and let data and recovery objectives drive disaster recovery.

38
00:05:02,694 --> 00:05:07,136
And build your platform as a product, with golden paths and self-service.

39
00:05:07,236 --> 00:05:15,214
Congratulations. You've completed all four levels of the Kubernetes course, from your first container to running platforms in production.
